[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which program should the preferences storage serve? → Browser app (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Browser app it is — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, scoped to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m/[38;5;153mapp.js[39m.

  Next: [1mwhat actually gets stored.[0m This decides whether the module has a known schema or is an open bag of keys, and
  that shapes validation, defaults, and migration.

  - [1mA fixed, known set[0m (e.g. [38;5;153musername[39m, [38;5;153mrememberMe[39m, [38;5;153mtheme[39m) — buys you real defaults, type validation, and a migration
    story when a key changes meaning. Costs a small edit to the module whenever you add a preference. Rules out
    consumers storing arbitrary junk, which is mostly a feature.
  - [1mA generic key/value store[0m ([38;5;153mprefs.get(key)[39m / [38;5;153mprefs.set(key, value)[39m) — buys you zero-friction additions. Costs
    typo-safety and defaults: nothing knows what a valid key is, so a misspelling silently reads [38;5;153mundefined[39m. Rules out
    schema-driven validation later without a breaking change.

  This one is moderately expensive to reverse: the schema decision is baked into the public API and into whatever is
  already written to users' browsers by then.

  [1mMy recommendation: a fixed set with declared defaults.[0m With one form on the page the key list is short and knowable,
  and declared defaults are what make "settings persist" behave sanely on a first visit or after a cache clear.

  One thing I'll flag regardless of your answer: [1mthe password must not be persisted.[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is plaintext, readable
  by any script on the origin, and survives logout. I'll design "remember me" to store the username only, and I'll
  write that constraint into the spec explicitly.
